+# Decision: Delivery approach – self-hosted WebSockets
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must push committed stall-status edits to up to 800 simultaneous browsers with changes visible within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, recover after up to 20 minutes offline, and retain seven days of revisions. Editors submit commands over HTTPS to a single authoritative board per event. The delivery mechanism must provide fast updates, reliable publication with replay for catch-up, and visible connectivity failures, while remaining compatible with self-hosted and managed infrastructure tenets.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Gates:
+- Meets freshness goal of ≤15s under normal connectivity.
+- Supports replayable revisions for catch-up after disconnection up to 20 minutes.
+- Maintains visible connectivity failures rather than silent staleness.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+- **major** – Vendor independence / cost predictability. Tenet: self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted; no vendor coupling is preferred for operational control.
+- **major** – Update latency under normal and burst load. Tenet: connectivity failures must be visible.
+- **moderate** – Operational burden for connection management and reconnects.
+- **moderate** – Repeated read load on change store.
+- **minor** – Time to implement initial delivery.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+Description: Browsers poll the change store over HTTPS every five seconds using cursor queries. Delivery stays request/response; the store needs cursor queries.
+
+Analysis:
+- Meets freshness goal: passes. Average lag ~2.5s plus processing, within 15s.
+- Supports replayable revisions: passes. Cursor queries can walk revision history.
+- Visible connectivity failures: partial. Failures are visible as failed requests, but silent staleness can be mistaken for no changes.
+- Vendor independence: strong. No vendor dependency.
+- Update latency: weak. Repeated reads increase load; latency grows under burst; opening-time bursts increase tail latency.
+- Operational burden: strong. Stateless operation; no connection management.
+- Repeated read load: weak. Polling creates repeated reads per browser, amplifying store load at scale.
+- Time to implement: strong. Simplest stateless operation.
+
+Summary: Simplest stateless operation with more repeated reads and a few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+Description: Server maintains WebSocket sessions per browser. Committed edits are published to the session; the change store provides replayable revisions and a publication outbox for catch-up. Delivery manages sessions, connection capacity, reconnects, and reliable publication. Browsers implement reconnect/catch-up logic.
+
+Analysis:
+- Meets freshness goal: passes. Subsecond push under normal connectivity.
+- Supports replayable revisions: passes. Publication outbox and revision history enable catch-up after disconnect.
+- Visible connectivity failures: strong. Socket close events make failures explicit.
+- Vendor independence: strong. Self-hosted; no recurring realtime vendor fees.
+- Update latency: strong. Fast updates without polling delay.
+- Operational burden: weak. Owner must manage connection capacity, reconnect storms, and session state.
+- Repeated read load: strong. No polling reads; store read on publish only.
+- Time to implement: partial. Requires session management and replay logic.
+
+Summary: Fast updates without realtime vendor bill, accepting connection and reconnect operations work.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+Description: Delivery wraps a hosted realtime provider. The store publishes changes to the provider for reconciliation; browsers use the provider's subscription model.
+
+Analysis:
+- Meets freshness goal: passes. Provider delivers low-latency updates.
+- Supports replayable revisions: partial. Depends on provider's replay/catch-up guarantees; reconciliation needed.
+- Visible connectivity failures: partial. Provider abstracts failures; visibility depends on wrapper.
+- Vendor independence: weak. Provider coupling and recurring fees.
+- Update latency: strong. Quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection ops.
+- Operational burden: strong. Provider manages connection capacity and reconnects.
+- Repeated read load: strong. No polling reads.
+- Time to implement: strong. Quickest initial route.
+
+Summary: Quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection ops, but recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets is selected.
+
+It passes all gates and scores strong on vendor independence and update latency — major criteria tied to operational control and freshness goals — while accepting a weaker operational burden for connection management and reconnects. The moderate criteria trade-off is acceptable: repeated read load is reduced versus polling, and implementation time is sufficient given the existing HTTPS command boundary.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet of self-hosted and managed infrastructure being permitted and the preference for vendor independence over reduced operational burden. It obligates the change store to provide replayable revisions and a publication outbox, delivery to own session management, connection capacity, reconnect handling, and reliable publication, and browser sync to implement subscription, reconnect/catch-up logic, and connectivity visibility.
+
+It forecloses hosted realtime vendor coupling and eliminates polling's repeated read load and lag.
